			<div class="content" id="post-content-4846869">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>a middle aged artist with a degenerative brain disease desperate to understand how the brain works and how to fix her own</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As much as I like this as a character motivation, it feels really out of place in a story about origami brains: like, even though it teleports brains to her, it also damages them in a way that almost certainly means she can't get any further insight from them; and it just&#8230;feels like it'd be a lot more useful for her to like&#8230;get the brains without damaging them? Ideally without even necessarily killing people?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A field agent stationed at a hospital notices something strange on an autopsy report: the deceased person had no brain and no signs of how it was taken</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this phrasing kinda implies that the person carrying out the autopsy doesn't notice it was strange? Like the whole way this unfolds kinda implies that somehow people don't notice this happening and it feels&#8230;idk, weirdly unsubtle? Like, it feels like death from your brain disappearing would feel&#8230; unexpected enough even in a hospital that they'd have to do autopsies and the absence of the brain there feels like a major detail worth flagging?</p>
<hr />
<p>Iunno, I'm really torn on this as a whole now: the whole angle of the villain is something that really resonates with me and which I really dig; but a lot of what they end up doing and how it works feels&#8230;iffy to me to the point where I'm not sure I could suspend my disbelief through the draft.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

